== WHAT MUTATION G SETTLES, AND IT IS THE REASON P-8b HOLDS ITS OWN COPY OF
== THE TABLE. Look at the counts line: 72 pairs, 14 legal, 58 illegal, 14
== legal allowed, 58 illegal refused, 0 wrong. A probe that asked the shipped
== function which pairs were legal and then checked that it refused the rest
== would have gone green over a transition table with REFUNDED to RELEASED in
== it, which is the single pair P-8 has been testing all week. Only the
== hand-written list of fourteen pairs in the probe caught it, as two
== disagreements.
==
== ONE DEFECT IN THE PROBE ITSELF, FOUND BY MUTATION G AND FIXED. The first
== version of the disagreement query read
== `A except B union all B except A` with no brackets. `except` and `union`
== share a precedence and associate leftwards, so Postgres read it as
== `((A except B) union all B) except A` and reported ONE disagreement where
== there were two. Every leaf is now parenthesised, and the re-run above is
== after that fix.
